        Implementation direction: preserve absolute per-thread clocks but separately account for inherited work, so a newly created thread contributes zero until it does new work. This avoids adding a host-timed scheduler barrier or reducing clock precision. The worker is implementing the small accounting change and regression controls while an independent reviewer checks clone, vfork, RPC, exec and replay paths. A separate Claude-family reviewer has read the full scheduler and source paper in preparation for the final diff. No reviewed or landed claim yet.
      [2026-09-14 19:22]
        Independent design review reproduced the trace diagnosis and found no need for a new scheduler barrier. The required accounting invariant is: global total equals epoch plus scheduler/external time plus each thread's latest absolute duration minus its stored inherited duration. Absolute per-thread time remains available to scheduler and exec/replay consumers. Vfork must carry the inherited baseline on its first creation RPC; exec must preserve the existing baseline rather than replacing it with freshly initialized local metadata. Implementation and regression work are in progress.
        
