        # Terminal cancellation and fork ownership repair
        
        Write destination is the registered slot /home/newton/work/dev-hermit/worktrees/slots/kvm-terminal-20260915-codex, branch codex/kvm-terminal-20260915, exact base 5f2ccf2167116248968f0abfd7cbf6cbb47280ea. Root confirmed registration and authorization before any product edits or builds. No clock-slot edits, commits, pushes, or public pull-request mutations are authorized.
        
        The independent P1 is confirmed by reading the actual owners. A subscribed callback releases its registered fork start gate before finish_cancelled_tool_thread. The child handle is owned by ElfExecutor.pending_processes, then sometimes completed_processes after a nonblocking wait collected status. The child keeps global Tool state through its backend lifecycle callback. Terminal cancellation currently never joins either process collection, although ordinary exits call join_all_child_processes. The latter returns early on the first error, and draining completed handles with an early return drops and detaches later handles. A lost start gate can also leave a pending handle in the map before the early error. notify_tool_exit's free helper returns immediately after a failing thread hook and does not attempt the consuming process hook.
        
        The intended bounded repair is to exhaust registered and completed process ownership, preserve original errors, and consume owner hooks before terminal API return. Cancellation must retain generation retirement and child start registration ordering. Child cleanup must continue after earlier failures, including lost start delivery, child panics, callback failures, and missing published status. Single original failures should retain their original error; multiple failures must retain all diagnostics in stable collection order. Normal exits using the same cleanup need corresponding error handling so an added question-mark call does not move the leak into the failure branch. This does not define new Linux orphan policy; it preserves the backend's existing wait-for-owned-children exit contract.
        
        Actual Tool tests will execute a small static ELF, inject fork from the parent's subscribed syscall callback, and cancel within that same callback. Host-controlled child gates establish a live child at cancellation and prevent it from finishing until the harness explicitly releases it. The harness always releases and joins its own worker even when testing the broken base. The public API must not return while that child is blocked; exact captured stdout/stderr, every consuming thread/process hook, child host-thread termination, and global-state release are checked at return.
        
        A completed-child control will let children publish status and then fail in the backend child-wait callback. Actual guest wait4(WNOHANG) moves their already-finished handles into completed_processes before parent cancellation. Both child errors must survive even though no child retains global state. Pending-child controls put an early failed child before a still-running later child, proving cleanup does not stop at the first failure. Owner thread/process hook failures are combined with child failures to require every diagnostic and every consuming hook. Ordinary exit controls and the existing live CLONE_THREAD terminal-cancellation fixture will run unchanged.
        
        The reproducer tests must fail with unchanged base production before the repair is applied. Additional low-level executor tests will cover lost gates, panics, missing completion, deferred errors, and complete draining where a real guest cannot directly manufacture those failures. All commands are bounded, four Cargo jobs, serial tests, retained failed attempts, exact source bindings; no unavailable KVM case is counted as success. Independent review and landing remain root-owned.
